-- Gates on links, and the reader anchor.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from src/server/db/schema.ts, then edited BY HAND to add
-- `IF NOT EXISTS`. Read the two notes below before changing anything here; both of them are about
-- running this file against a database that already has real rows in it.
--
-- WHY `IF NOT EXISTS` IS ADDED BY HAND, EVERY TIME.
-- drizzle-kit emits a bare `CREATE TABLE`. Three tables in a real PanelX database are created
-- outside the migration system, by `ensureLayoutTable()` in api/layout.ts and
-- `ensureSourceIndexTables()` in lib/sourceindex.ts, which run `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` at
-- import because db/schema.ts and drizzle/ belonged to another agent when those features were
-- built. Both files say so in their headers and both warn about exactly this: a generated bare
-- `CREATE TABLE` fails against every database those functions have already touched. `reader_anchors`
-- has no `ensure*` function today, but the next agent who cannot edit schema.ts will write one, and
-- this file is the pattern they will copy.
--
-- WHY THE TWO `ALTER TABLE` LINES HAVE NO SUCH GUARD, AND WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE TO RUN TWICE.
-- SQLite has no `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` and no conditional DDL, so there is no honest way to
-- write one. What makes a second run harmless is the migration journal: when drizzle applies this
-- file it writes a row into `__drizzle_migrations` carrying this migration's `when` value from
-- drizzle/meta/_journal.json, and afterwards it applies only files whose `when` is later than the
-- newest row there. src/server/db/migrate.ts checks that the journal exists before it runs
-- anything, and says so plainly when it does not, because the failure that guard prevents —
-- `table records already exists`, out of a command whose whole job is to be run again — is not a
-- sentence anybody can act on.
--
-- NOTHING BELOW REWRITES A ROW. Two nullable columns are appended to `links`, which SQLite does by
-- editing the table header alone, and one new table is created. No column is dropped, renamed or
-- retyped, no table is rebuilt, and every existing link keeps every value it had.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS `reader_anchors` (
	`workspace_id` text DEFAULT 'default' NOT NULL,
	`reader` text NOT NULL,
	`last_seen_at` text NOT NULL,
	`last_change_id` text,
	PRIMARY KEY(`workspace_id`, `reader`),
	FOREIGN KEY (`last_change_id`) REFERENCES `changes`(`id`) ON UPDATE no action ON DELETE no action
);
--> statement-breakpoint
-- Existing `blocks` links get NULL here, and NULL means UNQUALIFIED — the author said that one
-- thing blocks another and said nothing about how much. It is not a hard gate. See the column
-- comment in src/server/db/schema.ts for why reading it as one is the expensive mistake.
ALTER TABLE `links` ADD `gate` text;--> statement-breakpoint
